            <ArticleTitle>Role of endoscopic sonography in the diagnosis of a fish bone perforation of the gastric wall resulting in a submucosal pseudotumor.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>415-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/jcu.20817</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report the case of a 72-year-old woman with a fish bone-induced granulation tissue in the gastric wall that was initially diagnosed as a submucosal tumor by panendoscopy. High-frequency endoscopic ultrasonography identified the linear calcified fish bone embedded in a heterogeneous mass.</AbstractText>
